Confirmed: yes
Severity: high

## Impact
An attacker can craft a phishing link via the `/learn` endpoint that redirects users to arbitrary external domains (e.g., `https://malicious-site.com?url=https://evil.com/login`). Because the redirect originates from the trusted domain (`/learn`), users may trust the link and enter credentials on the attacker-controlled site, enabling credential theft. The invariant violated is *user trust in origin integrity* — the application delegates full redirect control to untrusted input without validation.

## Attack Scenario
1. Attacker constructs a URL: `https://victim-app.com/learn?url=https://evil.com/phish`.
2. User clicks the link while authenticated (e.g., via email phishing).
3. The `/learn` route (line 62–65 in `app/routes/index.js`) reads `req.query.url` and passes it directly to `res.redirect()` (Express’s `res.redirect()` accepts absolute URLs).
4. User is redirected to `https://evil.com/phish`, which mimics the login page.
5. User enters credentials, which are captured by the attacker.

## Recommendation
Primary fix: Validate and restrict redirect URLs to a known-safe allowlist of domains or relative paths. For Express, sanitize `req.query.url` before redirecting:

```js
app.get("/learn", isLoggedIn, (req, res) => {
    const url = req.query.url;
    if (!url || !url.startsWith('/')) {
        // Reject absolute URLs or non-relative paths
        return res.redirect('/dashboard');
    }
    // Optionally, further restrict to known internal paths
    if (!/^\/(learn|dashboard|profile|research|benefits|contributions|allocations|memos)$/i.test(url)) {
        return res.redirect('/dashboard');
    }
    return res.redirect(url);
});
```

Defense in depth: Set `X-Frame-Options: DENY` and `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'` to mitigate phishing impact; log redirect attempts for monitoring.

